WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.160
Friction always slows
the whole object down.

00:00:00.465 --> 00:00:01.288
True or false?

00:00:02.096 --> 00:00:05.287
It sounds familiar, but walking
needs friction to push us

00:00:05.287 --> 00:00:09.135
forwards, so that statement cannot
be right in every situation.

00:00:09.905 --> 00:00:10.603
Excellent.

00:00:11.001 --> 00:00:16.284
Friction opposes relative sliding or the
tendency to slide at the contact surfaces.

00:00:16.672 --> 00:00:20.315
When your foot pushes backwards against
the ground, static friction from

00:00:20.315 --> 00:00:22.235
the ground can propel you forwards.

00:00:23.037 --> 00:00:28.221
Sliding friction on a skidding box acts
against its sliding, but static friction

00:00:28.223 --> 00:00:30.445
can accelerate a person or a vehicle.

00:00:30.979 --> 00:00:34.125
Opposing slip is more accurate
than always opposing travel.

00:00:34.819 --> 00:00:35.607
Exactly.

00:00:35.956 --> 00:00:39.915
Surface interactions are more complicated
than just tiny bumps hooking together.

00:00:40.314 --> 00:00:44.729
Friction is useful for grip and braking
but can also cause wear and unwanted

00:00:44.743 --> 00:00:46.805
energy transfer to internal stores.

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:50.690
Drag acts against motion relative
to the surrounding fluid.

00:00:50.975 --> 00:00:54.690
It usually increases with speed,
with other factors comparable, and

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:56.675
depends on shape and exposed area.

00:00:57.429 --> 00:00:57.832
Good.

00:00:58.341 --> 00:01:00.174
Streamlining can reduce drag.

00:01:00.484 --> 00:01:03.217
Lubrication, bearings,
or wheels can reduce some

00:01:03.247 --> 00:01:04.944
unwanted friction in machinery.

00:01:05.321 --> 00:01:08.924
A parachute deliberately increases
drag to reduce falling speed.

00:01:09.657 --> 00:01:13.212
If a falling object's upward drag
eventually equals its downward

00:01:13.212 --> 00:01:15.489
weight, resultant force is zero.

00:01:15.868 --> 00:01:19.547
It can continue downwards at
terminal speed, not suddenly stop.

00:01:20.328 --> 00:01:20.889
Correct.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:23.947
Balance the forces, then
describe the motion.

00:01:24.379 --> 00:01:26.819
Do not assume balanced means motionless.

00:01:27.192 --> 00:01:30.377
Different shapes and conditions
can give different terminal speeds.

00:01:30.775 --> 00:01:33.834
A runner covers 100 meters in 20 seconds.

00:01:34.212 --> 00:01:35.690
What is the average speed?

00:01:36.496 --> 00:01:40.606
Total distance divided by total
time, five meters per second.

00:01:41.196 --> 00:01:44.093
That does not mean the runner
moved at exactly five meters

00:01:44.093 --> 00:01:45.696
per second at every moment.

00:01:46.406 --> 00:01:46.982
Correct.

00:01:47.596 --> 00:01:51.098
For a whole journey, include
stops in total elapsed time.

00:01:51.703 --> 00:01:54.826
Averages from separate parts
cannot simply be averaged unless

00:01:54.827 --> 00:01:56.142
the weighting is appropriate.

00:01:56.921 --> 00:02:01.808
If I travel 20 meters in five seconds
and then wait five seconds, the

00:02:01.808 --> 00:02:06.393
whole trip average is 20 divided
by 10, two meters per second.

00:02:07.084 --> 00:02:10.862
On a graph of cumulative distance
traveled against time, what

00:02:10.863 --> 00:02:12.500
does a horizontal section mean?

00:02:13.251 --> 00:02:16.637
No additional distance is covered,
so the object is stationary.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:21.627
A straight sloping section means constant
speed, and a steeper slope means greater

00:02:21.628 --> 00:02:23.889
speed if the axes use the same scale.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:25.470
Exactly.

00:02:25.941 --> 00:02:29.823
Gradient equals change in distance
divided by change in time.

00:02:30.231 --> 00:02:33.636
For example, a rise of 12
meters over three seconds

00:02:33.654 --> 00:02:35.251
gives four meters per second.

00:02:36.033 --> 00:02:37.896
The line is not a drawing of the road.

00:02:38.343 --> 00:02:41.182
A steep line does not mean the
runner climbed a steep hill.

00:02:41.961 --> 00:02:42.351
Good.

00:02:42.679 --> 00:02:44.126
Also read the axis wording.

00:02:44.391 --> 00:02:46.813
Cumulative distance
traveled cannot decrease.

00:02:47.358 --> 00:02:50.759
A graph of position or distance
from a particular starting point can

00:02:50.759 --> 00:02:54.411
decrease when the object returns,
which needs a different interpretation

00:02:54.793 --> 00:02:58.745
A spring starts 10 centimeters long
and becomes 14 centimeters long.

00:02:59.133 --> 00:03:00.384
What is its extension?

00:03:01.158 --> 00:03:02.941
Four centimeters, not 14.

00:03:03.407 --> 00:03:07.842
Extension is the increase in length
found by subtracting the original length.

00:03:08.613 --> 00:03:09.419
Exactly.

00:03:09.778 --> 00:03:13.283
In the proportional region,
Hooke's law says force equals

00:03:13.291 --> 00:03:15.347
spring constant times extension.

00:03:15.778 --> 00:03:18.840
Double the force and the
extension doubles, provided

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:20.305
we remain within that region.

00:03:21.073 --> 00:03:25.528
The graph of force against extension is
a straight line through the origin there.

00:03:25.887 --> 00:03:29.899
Its gradient is the spring constant
measured in newtons per meter.

00:03:30.724 --> 00:03:31.296
Correct.

00:03:31.495 --> 00:03:33.100
Be careful with two limits.

00:03:33.415 --> 00:03:37.258
The limit of proportionality is where
that straight line relationship ends.

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:40.566
The elastic limit concerns
whether the spring returns to its

00:03:40.569 --> 00:03:42.148
original length when unloaded.

00:03:42.367 --> 00:03:44.146
They are not necessarily identical.

00:03:44.885 --> 00:03:49.481
So it can stop being proportional before
it becomes permanently deformed, and

00:03:49.502 --> 00:03:53.204
an elastic band is not automatically
a perfect Hooke's law spring.

00:03:54.092 --> 00:03:54.935
Exactly.

00:03:55.338 --> 00:03:58.578
A calibrated spring balance
uses a predictable relation

00:03:58.578 --> 00:04:00.410
between force and extension.

00:04:01.082 --> 00:04:05.244
For a safe school investigation,
stay within its rated range, keep

00:04:05.245 --> 00:04:09.212
suspended masses controlled, and
record the unloaded length first.

00:04:09.733 --> 00:04:12.621
A 10 newton force acts with
a perpendicular distance of

00:04:12.623 --> 00:04:14.066
half a meter from a pivot.

00:04:14.362 --> 00:04:15.434
What is its moment?

00:04:16.218 --> 00:04:19.898
Force times perpendicular
distance, five newton meters.

00:04:20.531 --> 00:04:23.853
I must use the shortest perpendicular
distance from the pivot to

00:04:23.853 --> 00:04:25.297
the force's line of action.

00:04:26.041 --> 00:04:26.820
Exactly.

00:04:27.098 --> 00:04:30.319
A smaller force farther from
the pivot can produce a larger

00:04:30.319 --> 00:04:32.207
moment than a bigger force nearby.

00:04:32.794 --> 00:04:35.563
Door handles are placed away
from hinges for a reason.

00:04:36.358 --> 00:04:40.000
A 20 newton force half a meter to
one side can balance 10 newtons
